---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AuthorByline from '../components/AuthorByline.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Why Your Emails Go to Spam (14 Reasons)" description="Why emails go to spam: the 14 real causes from missing SPF/DKIM/DMARC to blacklistings and content triggers, with the fix for each.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Why Emails Go to Spam</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">Why Your Emails Go to Spam</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      Spam placement is almost never one thing: it is a scoring system weighing your authentication,
      your sending history, your content, and your infrastructure. Here are the causes that account
      for nearly everything, strongest first.
    </p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>Authentication (the big three)</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li><strong>Missing or broken SPF.</strong> Check with the <a href="/spf-record-checker/">SPF checker</a>; over the 10-lookup limit counts as broken.</li>
        <li><strong>No DKIM signature.</strong> Common after moving senders; verify the selector with the <a href="/dkim-record-checker/">DKIM checker</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>No DMARC, or p=none with failures unaddressed.</strong> The <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/">DMARC checker</a> grades you instantly.</li>
        <li><strong>Gmail/Yahoo 2024 sender requirements unmet.</strong> Bulk senders without aligned SPF/DKIM, DMARC, and one-click unsubscribe are increasingly rejected outright.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>Reputation</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5" start="5">
        <li><strong>Listing on a blocklist.</strong> Run the <a href="/blacklist-checker/">blacklist checker</a>; one major listing can junk everything.</li>
        <li><strong>No reverse DNS (PTR) on your sending IP.</strong> Set it with your host; match your HELO name.</li>
        <li><strong>New domain or IP with no warm-up.</strong> Volume before reputation = spam folder. Warm up gradually.</li>
        <li><strong>Shared IP with a noisy neighbor.</strong> Consider dedicated sending IP at volume.</li>
        <li><strong>Spam complaints above ~0.1%.</strong> List hygiene and expectation-setting fix this; nothing technical does.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>Content and behavior</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5" start="10">
        <li><strong>Spammy content patterns</strong>: excessive caps, deceptive subject lines, image-only bodies, URL shorteners.</li>
        <li><strong>Sudden volume spikes</strong> the sending history can't explain.</li>
        <li><strong>Stale lists</strong>: old permission, recycled addresses that convert to spam traps.</li>
        <li><strong>Broken links or links to suspicious domains</strong> in the body.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing unsubscribe</strong>, or one that is hard to use (now a compliance failure, not just a scoring one).</li>
        <li><strong>Sending from a domain with no website or MX</strong>: the domain itself looks disposable.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>The diagnostic order that works</h2>
      <p>
        Authenticate first (it is free and fast), then check listings, then inspect engagement. The
        homepage <a href="/">security scanner</a> covers the authentication block in one pass, and the
        <a href="/email-authentication/">email authentication hub</a> collects the deep guides.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AuthorByline />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
